// A `!` on a module const asks for real storage: a writable global that grows like
// any other array. The restriction is the POOL's, not the collector's — a permanent
// GC frame roots these globals, so a reallocated buffer is forwarded, not collected.
//
// A FLAT pool entry (primitive — `string` included, it is an interned u32 atom —
// enum, or a distinct over one) carries a storage class. An AGGREGATE entry does
// not: its struct elements are emitted as separate `static const` objects, so a
// mutable aggregate has two independent levels to decide (the slots, and the
// elements), and that surface stays closed. A bare struct or map const isn't pooled
// at all, so it has no storage to make writable. T3076 on each.
//
// The marker is rejected at ANY level, not just the array's: `Spec![]` promises no
// more than `Spec[]!` does, so it would only mislead.
module "snippet"

Spec :: struct { code: i32 }

SPECS:  Spec[]!:  [Spec { .code = 1 }]
GRID:   i32[][]!: [[1]]
ORIGIN: Spec!:    Spec { .code = 0 }
TPL:    Spec![]:  [Spec { .code = 2 }]

main :: fn() -> i32 = SPECS[0].code + GRID[0][0] + ORIGIN.code + TPL[0].code
